"""Criterion-specific walkthroughs and decision points for Understandable."""

WALKTHROUGHS = {
'3.1.1': (
'Walkthrough: auditing translated pages',
'Imagine a course catalogue with English and Filipino versions. Open the English page, inspect its root html element, and record the language tag. Follow the link to the Filipino version and inspect the root again. The text may change while the application shell remains mounted; if the root still says English, a screen reader that uses language information may apply an inappropriate pronunciation. Update the document language when the route changes, and ensure that a direct link and browser refresh produce the same correct result.',
'Next inspect the language selector itself. A control named “English” or “Filipino” identifies the choice, but that label does not establish the language of the destination document. Use a valid language tag representing the main language, and review any embedded passages that differ under the separate language-of-parts requirement. An automated rule that finds an attribute cannot determine whether the selected language matches the text; compare it with the content and listen when suitable speech voices are available.',
'If the page contains equally substantial sections in multiple languages, decide which language best describes the document as a whole and explicitly identify relevant changes in its parts. Record the content examined, the actual attribute, and whether navigation changed it. This makes the outcome reproducible and reveals errors that appear only after a client-side transition.'),
'3.1.2': (
'Walkthrough: a quotation in a multilingual lesson',
'Start with an English document whose root declares English. Insert a complete French quotation within an English paragraph, then put the French language tag on the element enclosing the quotation. The surrounding words inherit English from the document; the quotation inherits French from its enclosing element. Keep punctuation associated with the quoted passage within the same logical boundary so the markup describes the text rather than an arbitrary visual span.',
'Use browser inspection to verify the attribute is attached to the text that actually appears. With language switching enabled in a screen reader, listen before, during, and after the quotation. If the speech voice does not change, verify whether a suitable voice is installed before assuming that the markup failed. If French pronunciation continues into the next English sentence, inspect whether the French element accidentally wraps too much content.',
'Review common exceptions in context. Proper names, technical terms, words of indeterminate language, and words that have become part of the immediately surrounding language are treated differently by the criterion. Do not mark an entire English paragraph as French just because it contains one phrase, and do not assume a colored flag supplies a programmatic language. Record the boundaries and the speech configuration used for testing.'),
'3.1.3': (
'Walkthrough: defining a term in a security course',
'A lesson might say “The token expires after ten minutes.” A newcomer may know token as a physical object and not understand that this lesson uses it for a temporary credential. Introduce the intended meaning in the surrounding text: a token is a value that a service uses to recognize or authorize a request. Explain expiration in the next sentence, including what the learner should do when it occurs.',
'If the term appears across many lessons, add a glossary entry and a descriptive link. Test the link from the keyboard and make sure the learner can return to the sentence without losing context. A glossary that lists several unrelated meanings but does not identify the sense used in the lesson leaves the original ambiguity unresolved. An icon with an unexplained tooltip also fails to provide a dependable route for every user.',
'Now review idioms and phrases used in a restricted meaning, not just technical nouns. Ask a reader unfamiliar with the domain to explain a representative instruction in their own words. Revise the definition if it requires further unexplained jargon. Confirm that the definition is accurate for this course rather than promising a universal meaning that the term does not have.'),
'3.1.4': (
'Walkthrough: introducing abbreviations in documentation',
'A page instructs learners to call an API, but a reader arriving from search may never have seen the expansion. On the first relevant occurrence in that page, write “application programming interface (API)” and explain its role in the task. If multiple abbreviations share the same letters, make the intended expansion explicit rather than linking to a list of unrelated possibilities.',
'For a long manual, a searchable glossary can supplement first-use explanations. Each entry should identify the abbreviation and its meaning in context. A table of expansions must have understandable headings and be reachable through a descriptive link. Check repeated interface labels too: a compact menu item may be the reader’s first encounter even when the document text explains the term later.',
'Try the page with a mouse, keyboard, and touch input. A browser tooltip shown only on hover is easy to miss and may not be consistently exposed by assistive technologies; do not rely on it as the only mechanism. Check the criterion’s exceptions for abbreviations that have become part of the language instead of mechanically expanding every shortened word.'),
'3.1.5': (
'Walkthrough: rewriting an enrollment instruction',
'Consider a paragraph that combines eligibility, a deadline, a document requirement, and a consequence for late submission in one long sentence. Identify each obligation before simplifying. A plain-language alternative might explain who can apply, list the required document, give the deadline with its time zone, and describe what happens after submission. A shorter paragraph that omits the deadline is easier to read but not a faithful explanation.',
'Use concrete verbs and define specialized terms at the point they are needed. Break a sequence into logical steps and show a worked example if that helps the learner understand how a rule applies. Preserve important exceptions and distinguish a requirement from a recommendation. If a diagram communicates the process, provide its information in accessible text as well so that the supplemental material is not limited to vision.',
'Ask a learner unfamiliar with the process to locate the deadline and explain the next action using the alternative. Compare their answers with the original and revise unclear wording. A readability calculator can flag difficult passages, but it cannot establish that the required details are present or that readers can complete the task. The WCAG criterion is conditional and includes treatment of proper names and titles; evaluate the actual text.'),
'3.1.6': (
'Walkthrough: identifying an ambiguous pronunciation',
'Write two sentences using “record”: one asks a learner to record a video, while the other refers to a record of the result. The word has different pronunciation patterns in those uses. Ask whether the surrounding sentence already makes the intended meaning clear. The criterion is concerned with cases where meaning is ambiguous unless the pronunciation is known, not with adding guides to every word with several pronunciations.',
'Where a guide is needed, connect it to the exact occurrence. A written guide can describe the stressed syllable or use a notation appropriate for the intended audience; an audio example can supplement that explanation. Make a linked guide discoverable and operable with keyboard and touch, and avoid requiring a reader to interpret a notation they have not been taught.',
'Test the explanation with someone who has not seen the example before. Can they distinguish the intended action from the noun? If audio is present, verify the recording is clear and the control is labeled; ensure a usable text-based explanation is available for a learner who cannot hear it. Consult language expertise where the pronunciation or meaning is uncertain.'),
'3.2.1': (
'Walkthrough: repairing a focus-triggered redirect',
'A menu developer attaches navigation to a link’s focus event so that a destination opens as soon as the link receives keyboard focus. Starting at the top of the page, press Tab until that link is reached. The page changes before Enter is pressed, preventing the learner from examining the remaining options. This is a different action from a visible focus outline or a brief description appearing near the control.',
'Remove the redirect from the focus handler. Keep a semantic link with a real destination, and allow the browser’s normal activation behavior to follow it when the user chooses the link. If JavaScript is needed, attach the consequential operation to activation rather than focus. Check that clicking still works and that keyboard focus remains visible while moving through the menu.',
'Test Tab and Shift+Tab across the whole menu and verify that the address, open window, and focused element do not change context merely because focus moved. Then activate the intended link and confirm the destination. Repeat after a dialog closes or validation returns focus to a field, because programmatically moved focus can also trigger a problematic handler.'),
'3.2.2': (
'Walkthrough: a language selector',
'Suppose a selector immediately navigates to another language version whenever its value changes. A keyboard user may press an arrow key while exploring the options and unexpectedly leave the page. Replace the immediate redirect with a labeled selector and an Apply button, or provide clear advance information about an immediate context change where the intended design requires one.',
'Test from the keyboard without activating Apply. Move through several options, return to the original choice, and check that the current page and focus remain stable. Then choose a language and activate Apply; verify that the selected language and destination agree. Keep the label and any behavioral instructions available before the user operates the control.',
'A live update is not automatically a change of context. For instance, updating an estimated price in place without navigating or moving focus is a different interaction, though it still needs clear communication and accessible status handling. Review the behavior experienced by the learner, not simply whether the code uses an input or change event.'),
'3.2.3': (
'Walkthrough: reviewing a set of course pages',
'Record the header links on the course overview, lesson, and account pages. If Home, Courses, and Help are repeated, note their relative order on each page. A lesson page may insert a “Resources” link between Courses and Help without changing the relative sequence of the three repeated links. A page that moves Help ahead of Home changes the sequence a learner has already learned.',
'Check both desktop and narrow mobile layouts. Open the collapsed menu and use the keyboard to record the order in which repeated links receive focus. A visual layout and the DOM or focus order can diverge, so inspecting only a screenshot is not enough. Compare pages in the same set, and distinguish a user’s explicit customization from an unexplained site-wide change.',
'When fixing an inconsistency, adjust the shared navigation template if possible so the correction applies across pages. Recheck page-specific additions to make sure they do not reverse the repeated links. Keep a record of the pages and viewport sizes inspected. This criterion concerns consistent relative order; it does not require an identical set of links on every page.'),
'3.2.4': (
'Walkthrough: auditing repeated search controls',
'On one page a magnifying-glass button opens search and is named “Search”; on another, the same action is represented by the same icon but announced as “Find resources.” Compare the action, visible text, and accessible name across both pages. Decide whether the identification is understandable and consistent in context rather than judging the icon in isolation.',
'Fix a button that has no meaningful accessible name by providing an appropriate label. If visible text is also present, make sure its wording and the accessible name work together. A user who asks speech software to activate a visible control should not have to guess an unrelated programmatic name. Where the icon opens a different action on another page, use identification that distinguishes the functions.',
'Test the repeated component on all relevant templates, including the compact mobile navigation. Ask a learner to find the search action on a second page after learning it on the first. Inspect the accessibility tree and activate each control to verify what it actually does. Consistency of placement is related but belongs to a separate navigation requirement; a consistent label alone does not establish correct placement.'),
'3.2.5': (
'Walkthrough: stopping automatic progression',
'A training page opens the next lesson ten seconds after a video ends. A learner who needs additional time to read the transcript may be moved before finishing. Provide an obvious Next action, or offer a control that lets the learner turn off automatic progression. Make the current setting clear and do not make the learner race a timer to find it.',
'Inspect other possible triggers as well. A redirect may be scheduled by a timer, fired by focus, caused by changing a select value, or initiated after a background request finishes. Trace what happens to the page, focus, and browser window. An in-place update of a small status message is not automatically equivalent to replacing the document; decide whether the change affects context in the relevant sense.',
'Test with automatic progression enabled and disabled. Confirm that disabling it persists for the promised scope, that the control works from the keyboard, and that the learner can still choose Next. Record which transition occurred, what initiated it, and whether the user had an accessible mechanism to prevent it. This enhanced criterion is broader than the focus-only and input-only requirements.'),
'3.2.6': (
'Walkthrough: comparing help locations',
'A course site shows a support link after the main navigation on the overview page. On the lesson and assessment pages, the same link appears before the logo. A learner who relies on a familiar sequence must search again at the moment they need help. Place repeated help mechanisms in a consistent relative order across the relevant pages, unless the learner has initiated a change.',
'Make an inventory of the repeated mechanisms: a contact link, a telephone number, a self-help page, or an automated contact method may each be relevant. Compare like with like. The rule does not require every page to invent a support option that the site never offered; it concerns the position of the specified mechanisms when they repeat.',
'Test the mobile menu and keyboard reading sequence as well as the desktop layout. Record the item immediately before and after the support mechanism on each representative page, then navigate to the help itself to make sure the link is usable. Consistent placement helps people locate support, but it does not guarantee that the support content will solve the underlying issue.'),
'3.3.1': (
'Walkthrough: submitting an incomplete form',
'A learner submits a registration form without an email address and with an invalid date. A red outline alone does not tell someone who cannot perceive the color which input needs attention or why. Present text naming each error, such as “Enter an email address” and “Enter a valid date.” Keep messages near their controls and consider a summary that links to each affected field.',
'Associate the local message programmatically with the corresponding input and preserve the user’s other answers. When errors are added dynamically, test how they are announced and where focus goes; the learner should be able to discover the summary and return to the field. A summary does not replace the need for an intelligible message when the learner reaches the input.',
'Correct one field and submit again. Verify that the fixed error disappears or updates, the remaining error is still accurate, and no unrelated input was erased. Test a server-rejected value as well as client-side checks. Error identification explains what went wrong; the separate error-suggestion requirement concerns suitable advice on how to fix it when such advice is known and safe.'),
'3.3.2': (
'Walkthrough: building a date field',
'Give the input a visible label such as “Appointment date” and associate that label with the control. Explain the accepted format before entry, for example “Use day/month/year, such as 25/04/2026,” when the implementation actually expects that format. If the date is required, communicate that in text or another accessible manner rather than relying on an unexplained colored symbol.',
'A placeholder can illustrate a format, but it may disappear as the user types and does not reliably replace a persistent label or instruction. Connect supporting instructions to the input programmatically and check the accessible name and description. For a group of radio buttons, use a group question and individual option labels so that each choice makes sense when encountered independently.',
'Test the form without a mouse and with a screen reader. Before entering anything, can the learner identify the field, determine the expected value, and understand whether it is mandatory? Enter a valid example and verify that the application accepts exactly what the instructions promise. Revisit the guidance whenever validation changes.'),
'3.3.3': (
'Walkthrough: repairing a rejected date',
'A form displays “Invalid input” after a learner enters 31/02/2026. The message signals a failure but does not explain how to proceed. If the application can tell that the date is impossible, explain that the day must exist in the chosen month and give a valid example. If the issue is the expected format, provide the format that the validation actually accepts.',
'Keep the entered value and make the suggestion available near the input. Programmatically associate it with the field so a learner returning to the control can find it. Test both an impossible date and a valid date in the wrong format: one generic suggestion may not help both cases. After a correction, ensure the message no longer contradicts the current value.',
'The criterion applies when a correction is known, unless providing it would jeopardize security or the purpose of the content. A login system may give a general credential error rather than revealing whether an account exists. Document why a suggestion is withheld where applicable, and check that an accessible error identification is still present.'),
'3.3.4': (
'Walkthrough: reviewing a financial submission',
'A learner is about to pay a course fee. The review screen displays the amount, recipient, and chosen payment method, with a way to edit each before a final confirmation button. Enter an incorrect amount deliberately, continue to review, and correct it. Confirm that returning to edit does not remove other entries and that the corrected value appears on the final review.',
'The criterion allows specified alternatives: a submission may be reversible, input may be checked for errors with an opportunity to correct them, or the user may review, confirm, and correct before finalizing. Evaluate the mechanism actually provided. A confirmation dialog that only asks “Continue?” without revealing the important values may not enable meaningful review.',
'If reversal is claimed, test the real reversal path and any time limit; a vague instruction to contact support is not evidence that reversal works. If error checking is used, test an error that the application is capable of detecting. Identify whether the transaction falls in the criterion’s covered legal, financial, user-controlled data, or test-response situations, and document the specific safeguard observed.'),
'3.3.5': (
'Walkthrough: help for a username field',
'A registration form rejects a username but the learner cannot tell which characters are allowed. Add concise guidance adjacent to the field, such as the real permitted length and character rules. If the policy is complex, link to a detailed explanation with a clear name while retaining the most important rules next to the input.',
'Test the help before entering a value and after an error. A keyboard user should be able to reach any help link, read its content, and return to the form without losing their work. If help opens as a dialog, check that focus moves into it, the contents are readable, and closing it returns the learner to a sensible location. Avoid relying on a hover-only icon.',
'Compare every example with the actual server validation. A sample containing a character that the backend rejects undermines the help even if the help is technically reachable. Ask someone unfamiliar with the form to choose a valid username using only the available instructions. Context-sensitive help addresses the current task; a generic support page with no guidance for the field may leave the learner searching.'),
'3.3.6': (
'Walkthrough: safeguarding a profile change',
'A learner changes a profile setting and presses Save. Choose a safeguard appropriate to the action: allow a reliable Undo, check for detectable input errors and allow corrections, or offer a review and confirmation step. A routine form may not be covered by the narrower categories of the related AA criterion, but this enhanced criterion extends safeguards to submissions involving user input.',
'If Undo is used, make it discoverable and test its actual effect. Change the value, save it, activate Undo, refresh the page, and verify that the previous value was restored. If the restoration fails, communicate that failure instead of reporting success. A visual change that is not persisted is not a reliable reversal.',
'For validation, submit a detectable invalid value and verify that it can be corrected. For a review step, change a value during review and verify that the final submitted data contains the correction. Do not add a meaningless confirmation screen merely to create an extra click; the learner needs a practical opportunity to prevent or repair a mistake.'),
'3.3.7': (
'Walkthrough: shipping and billing details',
'A checkout asks for a shipping address, then requests a billing address in a later step of the same process. Offer “Use shipping address” as a selectable option or prefill the previously supplied information. Show the carried-forward value so the learner can inspect it. If billing is different, make changing the value straightforward instead of forcing both addresses to match.',
'Test three paths: reuse the existing address unchanged, edit a copied address, and enter a different address. Move back and forward through the steps and check that the final order contains the chosen values. Pay particular attention to whether editing the billing address accidentally changes the original shipping address.',
'Review the criterion’s exceptions before concluding that re-entry is a failure. Re-entry can be essential, required for security, or justified because the previous information is no longer valid. These are reasons to evaluate in the actual process, not a blanket reason to require repeated typing everywhere. Protect sensitive information when displaying a reused value and distinguish a new process from steps in the same one.'),
'3.3.8': (
'Walkthrough: mapping a sign-in journey',
'Trace a learner from account identifier to password, second factor, failed login, and recovery. At each step, list any task involving memory, transcription, or another cognitive function test. Check whether the learner can use an alternative that avoids the test or an assisting mechanism. For a password field, password-manager fill and paste can support a user who cannot reliably memorize or transcribe the value.',
'Test those mechanisms rather than assuming they work because the browser offers them. Attempt password-manager fill and paste into the actual fields, submit the result, and verify that application code does not block or alter it. Repeat for a one-time code where relevant. Check that the learner can find and operate any alternative without first passing the inaccessible test.',
'The minimum criterion has specified exceptions for recognizing objects and identifying personal content. Assess the exact challenge and its context; the exceptions do not mean that every visual puzzle is usable by every learner. A complete evaluation includes recovery and required second factors, since an accessible first password screen does not help if a mandatory later step blocks the same learner.'),
'3.3.9': (
'Walkthrough: checking the enhanced requirement',
'A service offers password-manager compatible sign-in, but account recovery requires the learner to identify a personal photograph. Evaluate recovery as part of the authentication journey. The enhanced criterion does not include the object-recognition and personal-content exceptions found in the minimum criterion. If that step uses a cognitive function test, an alternative without the test or an assisting mechanism is needed.',
'List each available route and the assumptions it makes. A passkey can reduce a memory burden when the learner has an enrolled device and can operate it, but the review must also consider the route available when that device is lost. A route advertised as an alternative is ineffective if its setup itself requires completing an inaccessible mandatory challenge.',
'Exercise enrollment, ordinary sign-in, second factor, and recovery using representative devices. Verify password-manager and paste behavior where applicable and record what actually happens at each step. Do not assume a technology name proves conformance: the result depends on the implementation and the complete set of required authentication steps.'),
}
